e  outcomes they know that if neither of  them confesses the police will not be  able to make the charge stick but they  will be interrogated in the cells for an  um another three months before the  police give up and let them go so if  neither confesses they actually get the  best outcome overall if you think about  the total aggregate each of them gets  three months each of them loses  essentially one quarter of a year but he  says if one of them confesses  implicating the other the one who  confesses will be released immediately  they go free they have no penalty  whatsoever but the other will be  sentenced to eight years in jail so  that's a pretty significant increase in  the  amount of time you can see why there  would be an incentive there to turn in  your fellow prisoner to confess and let  the burden fall upon them what happens  if both of them confess well then they  both get five years which is not a good  outcome right and actually the both  confess if you think about it in terms  of the aggregate it's the worst  altogether because it's ten years total  in the case of one of them confessing  and the other one not it's pretty bad  but you get to shift the burden entirely  on to the other person or if you happen  to be the sucker who behaved  altruistically while the other person  behaved egoistically that is selfishly  you get stuck with the 8-year burden so  that's the classical framework of the  problem and singer points out something  that you know is not unique to him of  course this is often used to demonstrate  the problems of egoism so he says that  each prisoner could ask themself this  this sort of line of reasoning well if  the other prisoner confesses it'll be  better for me if I've also confessed  because then I'll get five years instead  of eight and the other prisoner if they  don't confess it'll still be better for  me because I'll get released immediately  instead of being interrogated for  another three months because we're  interrogated 