e wind I  believe he said something like that I  maybe miss phrasing it but it's the same  idea you can't rely on a covenant or  agreement or pact unless there's some  way to enforce it so he says without  sanctions to back it up an agreement is  unable to bring two self-interested  individuals to the outcome that's best  for both of them what has to be changed  then well it's not by adding another  agreement we have to change the  motivations of these people if we can do  that then we can get a better solution  so like you says we have to change the  assumption that the prisoners are  motivated by self-interest alone if he  says for example they are altruistic to  the extent of caring as much for the  entry  their fellow prisoner as they care for  their own interests they will reason  thus if the other prisoner doesn't  confess will be better for both of us if  I don't confess because then we'll be in  total for prison for a total of six  months whereas if I do confess the total  will be eight years and if the other  prisoner does does confess it will still  be better if I don't confess that a  total served up to eight years instead  of ten so whatever happens  taking our interest together it'll be  better if I don't confess now notice he  doesn't say this has to be a altruistic  person in the sense of being totally  self-sacrificing you just have to care  as much about the other person's welfare  or outcomes as you do for your own and  if that's the case then the rational  thing for both people to do independent  of what the other person does not  knowing what the other person is going  to do is to not confess and to attain  the better position so altruism can help  us out of the prisoner's dilemma singer  says that's not the only way that we  could get ourselves out of the dilemma  says another possibility is that the  prisoners or what he calls conscientious  meaning that they regard it as morally  wrong to inform on a fellow prisoner or  if they're if they're able to make an  agreement they might believe they have a  duty to keep their promises in any case  there's something de ontological rather  than consequentialist going on there  they feel that once they've made an  agreement or you know that they're in a  situation like that the right thing to  do is to not confess and we don't worry  about what they're up you know their  reason for that obligation is it could  be that you know they've just been  brought up in a very strict code that  says outsiders we don't talk to them  doesn't matter that would lead them to  the same solution as the people who are  genuinely altruistic now what's the OP  shot for this we're not often in secret  prison cells thank goodness and actually  we're not usually in the situation that  singer is going to talk about you know  in this mythical past but let's look at  that so he says we we can use this to  explain and evolu  Machinery advantage and being genuinely  altruistic instead of making reciprocal  exchanges on the basis of calculated  self-interest suppose to early humans  are attacked by a saber-toothed cat  right and it doesn't have to be a  saber-toothed cat could be whatever you  like  could be a crocodile could be some you  know other terrifying thing what can  they do the the instead of being  confessing and not confessing it'll be  sticking around with your comrade and  fighting the thing or taking off if both  of them stick around and fight then  there is a chance that one of them will  get wounded but they'll probably Drive  the predator often they'll probably be  both okay if they both flee they don't  actually both come out bad because the  saber-tooth tiger is only hungry for you  know one belly full of person and we'll  get one of them fairly randomly if one  of them sticks around and the other one  doesn't that one who sticks around is  screwed they're gonna get killed by the  saber-tooth cat so there's the the the  structure of the dilemma he says from a  self-interested point of view if your  partner flees your chances are survival  are better if you flee to you have a 50%  chance rather than none at all and if  your partner stands and fights you still  do better to run you're sure of escape  if you flee whereas it's only probable  not certain that you and your partner  can overcome the cat so to purely  self-interest that early humans would  flee and one of them would die which is  you know bad fur for both Oh in the long  run really so he says to early humans  who cared for each other whether it be  again from altruism valuing the other  person's welfare equal to your own or  having some sort of moral code that  tells you what to do and makes you feel  bad if you don't do it either one of  those would lead to the better outcome  he says they would stand and fight and  most likely neither would die so he says  in if situations analogous to this  imaginary saber-toothed cat attack were  common early humans would do better hunt  with altruistic comrades than with  self-interested partners of course an  egoist who could find an altruist to go  hunting with him would do better still  but altruists who could not detect and  refused to assist purely self-interested  partners would be selected again so that  evolution itself would lead to people  who are prone to be altruistic and  support their partners at least when  confronted by danger so this is singers  analysis of the prisoner's dilemma as it  would apply to egoism and altruism  particularly reciprocal altruism in in  dangerous situations but I think it  could be applied to a lot of other  things as well and he's trying to argue  that this is something that's rooted in  our background in our evolution as the  kind of animal that we are  you
 

